p.src:
An annuity is a finite payment stream. It pays $x$ each period from $t=1$ to $t=T$, where $T$ is the last payment date. The lecture writes the final formula on the board, but its logic is especially clear if we derive it from the consol. An annuity is just a perpetuity with its tail removed: $\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{PDV}_{\text{annuity}}
&= \frac{x}{r}-\frac{x}{r(1+r)^T} \\
&= \frac{x}{r}\left(1-\frac{1}{(1+r)^T}\right).\end{aligned}$ That is the formula Shiller arrives at in the annuity panel. It is important not only as algebra but as a building block. The lecture’s concrete example is the traditional home mortgage: fixed payments for a finite horizon and then no more.

