The short one-period bond implies a payment of $\pounds 100$ in 1926. That is exactly the amount we are prepared to invest at that date. The long two-year position, scaled as above, pays $\pounds 100\cdot \frac{(1+r_2)^2}{1+r_1}$ in 1927. Therefore the gross return from 1926 to 1927 is $\begin{aligned}
1+f &= \frac{(1+r_2)^2}{1+r_1}, \\
f &= \frac{(1+r_2)^2}{1+r_1}-1.\end{aligned}$ That is the one-step forward-rate identity written on the board.

